🚽 Test case: round-22 review finding #3 -- `void *` is a real value
🚽 (round 21), but that doesn't make `*(void *)` valid: a pointer needs a
🚽 pointee TYPE to know what representation/size to read or write, and
🚽 VAR_VOID has none. The dereference-LHS check inside semantic_visit_
🚽 assignment() only verified pointer_level > 0 (true for `void *`),
🚽 never that the dereference wouldn't land at bare (VAR_VOID, 0). This
🚽 is a SEPARATE check from the general NODE_UNARY_OPERATION case's own
🚽 (same-named) fix, because an assignment's dereference LHS is
🚽 deliberately only partially walked by semantic_analyze_with_scope_
🚽 tracking()'s own NODE_ASSIGNMENT case (to avoid double-reporting the
🚽 pointer-ness check) -- meaning semantic_visit_assignment() is the
🚽 ONLY place that validates a dereference LHS's pointee type. Previously
🚽 reached only ast.c's write_value_to_address(), whose switch has no
🚽 VAR_VOID case, landing in "Unsupported assignment type" -- a runtime
🚽 symptom for a statically-knowable error.
skibidi main {
    skibidi *p = test_ptr_source();
    *p = 42;
    bussin 0;
}
